This week's reading introduced me to what research questions and a prospectus should look like. One of my early ideas for a research question was "how does paid promotion affect social media engagement" but this question doesn't meet several criteria for a research question. First, it's not specific. It doesn't define what social media platform I'm studying, what qualifies as paid promotions, or what type of engagement I'm looking at. Second, it's not measurable. It doesn't specify a measure of engagement. Third, it may be too broad to tackle within the given time frame. An idea I have for a better research question is: "does comment content and quantity on beauty influencer posts vary between Youtube videos and Instagram photos?" An alternative could be: "does social media post type have a relationship with comment content within the online beauty community?" These questions are based on the media richness theory. Video media is more rich than photo or text media, so commentors may respond differently based on that. These questions are more specific and less broad, narrowing down exactly what relationship will be studied. They still don't specify what exactly will be measured when it comes to comment content, however, which is something I need to decide before submitting research questions.
